/* book Worked example 2: Q: The coil of the first example (L = 6 mH) has primary resistance 3 Ω on a 12 V supply. A four-cylinder four-stroke engine's distributor cam has 4 lobes and a dwell of 54° per cylinder. Find the dwell time, the primary current at break and the stored energy at 3000 and at 6000 rpm.
   1. Full current V ÷ R = 12 ÷ 3 = 4 A; τ = L ÷ R = 0.006 ÷ 3 = 2 ms.
   2. At 3000 rpm the distributor turns at 1500 rpm: tdwell = 54 ÷ (6 × 1500) = 0.006 s = 6 ms.
   3. i = 4 × (1 − e−6/2) = 4 × (1 − 0.0498) = 3.80 A; E = 0.5 × 0.006 × 3.80² = 43.3 mJ.
   4. At 6000 rpm: tdwell = 54 ÷ (6 × 3000) = 3 ms; i = 4 × (1 − e−1.5) = 4 × 0.777 = 3.11 A; E = 0.5 × 0.006 × 3.11² = 29.0 mJ.
   Answer: 6 ms, 3.80 A and 43.3 mJ at 3000 rpm; 3 ms, 3.11 A and 29.0 mJ at 6000 rpm. Doubling the speed cuts the spark energy by a third. */
