/* book: lead: Spark timing is the instant the plug fires. Spark advance, also called ignition advance, is how many crank degrees before top dead centre (TDC) the spark occurs. The flame needs time to burn the charge, so the spark must come early enough for peak pressure to arrive just after TDC. As speed rises the same burn time covers more crank degrees, so advance must rise; at high load it must fall to avoid */
/* book Figure 2.11.1: The worked example on a crank-angle scale. With peak pressure held at 14° after TDC, the 4.0 ms burn at 1500 rpm needs a spark at 22°; at 3000 rpm turbulence cuts the burn to 2.5 ms, so 31° is enough, while a burn that stayed at 4.0 ms would need 58°. */
