/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Spark advance θ is the crank angle before TDC at which the spark fires.
   - θ is the advance (° before TDC); N is the speed (rpm); tb is the time from spark to peak pressure (s); θpk is the target peak position (° after TDC). 6N is the crank speed in degrees per second.
   - The burn time is not fixed: more turbulence at higher speed makes the flame faster, so tb falls, and the advance rises with speed but less than in proportion.
   - Older distributors used centrifugal and vacuum advance.
   - Modern engines use an ECU spark map of advance against speed and load (from manifold pressure or air flow).
   - High load needs less advance to avoid knock; light load needs more because the diluted charge burns slowly.
*/
